<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hedwig of Silesia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1174">1174</a>–<a href="/years/1243">1243</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Saint Hedwig of Silesia, Jadwiga of Silesia, Hedwig of Andechs · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">House of Andechs</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hedwig of Silesia was a daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">counts of Andechs</a>, dukes of Merania, and wife of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry I the Bearded</a> of Silesia. With her husband she founded the Cistercian convent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebnica">Trzebnica</a> and supported German settlement and new towns. Known for her piety and care for the poor, she was canonised in 1267 and became the patron saint of Silesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia.jpg" alt="Hedwig of Silesia" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hedwig_von_Schlesien.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hedwig was born in 1174 at Andechs Castle in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, a daughter of Count Berthold IV of Andechs, margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> and Istria, and his second wife Agnes of Wettin. Her sister Agnes married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>, a marriage annulled in 1200, and her sister Gertrude, killed in 1213, married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II of Hungary</a>, so that Hedwig was the aunt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Elizabeth of Hungary</a>. Her youngest sister Matilda became abbess of the Benedictine Abbey of Kitzingen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, where Hedwig received her education. At the age of twelve she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry I the Bearded</a>, heir of the Piast duke Boleslaus the Tall of Silesia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Henry succeeded his father in 1201, and Hedwig became Duchess of Silesia. He struggled with his Piast relatives, first with his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-iv-tanglefoot">Mieszko IV Tanglefoot</a>, who seized the Upper Silesian Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>. In 1229 Henry was captured at Płock Castle by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a>, and Hedwig went to Płock to plead for him and obtained his release. After the death of Władysław III Spindleshanks in 1231 Henry became Duke of Greater Poland, and the next year High Duke at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, the first Silesian Piast to hold both Silesia and the Seniorate Province in accord with the 1138 testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a>.</p><p>At Hedwig's request Henry established the Cistercian convent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebnica">Trzebnica</a> in 1202. The couple supported the donation of an Augustinian provostry at Nowogród Bobrzański and a commandery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> at Oleśnica Mała, and Hedwig founded several hospitals for the sick and lepers and gave her fortune to the Church. According to legend she went barefoot even in winter, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> urged her to wear shoes she carried them in her hands. After Henry died in 1238 and was buried at Trzebnica she moved into the convent, taking a lay sister's habit without vows, and invited German religious and settlers who founded towns and villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hedwig saw her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnica">Battle of Legnica</a> in 1241, during the first Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, after which Silesia fragmented into several Piast duchies. She died on 15 October 1243 and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebnica">Trzebnica</a> with her husband. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iv">Pope Clement IV</a> canonised her in 1267, at the suggestion of her grandson Władysław, Prince-Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>. She is the patron saint of Silesia, and her feast day is 16 October. In 1773 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> had St. Hedwig's built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> for Catholic Upper Silesian immigrants, and it has been the cathedral of the Archdiocese of Berlin since 1930. In March 2020 her remains, missing for centuries, were reported found in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> casket at Trzebnica.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hedwig of Silesia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Berthold IV's daughters married widely: Agnes became Queen of France, Gertrude Queen of Hungary and mother of Saint Elizabeth, and <b>Hedwig</b> Duchess of Silesia, later Saint Hedwig.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-merania">Duchy of Merania</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry I the Bearded and his wife <b>Saint Hedwig</b> invited German settlers, founded towns under German law and monasteries such as Trzebnica.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Duchy of Silesia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Burg Andechs, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebnica">Trzebnica</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.98,11.18&amp;year=1218" aria-label="Burg Andechs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hedwig of Silesia"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5636 5967h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5636 5967h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.98,11.18&amp;year=1218">What was here in 1218? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
